---
import ContentLayout from '../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import HeroSection from '../components/HeroSection.astro';
import WarningBox from '../components/WarningBox.astro';
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Failure-First Alignment Manifesto | Failure-First"
  description="Why failure should be the primary object of study in AI safety evaluation, not an edge case."
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Manifesto" }]}
>
  <HeroSection
    title="The failure-first<br /><em>manifesto</em>"
    subtitle="In a world of embodied AI, safety emerges from well-designed failure"
    animation="drift"
  />

  <section>
    <h2>Thesis</h2>
    <p>
      Alignment that only optimizes for correct task completion is brittle.
      Systems operate across time, across other agents, and&mdash;when embodied&mdash;across
      physical space that does not roll back. They will fail. The question is
      <strong>how</strong>, and whether anyone can tell before it costs something real.
    </p>
    <p>
      This manifesto argues that failure should be the <strong>primary object of
      study</strong> in AI safety evaluation&mdash;not an edge case, not a corner case,
      but the central concern. It also argues something narrower and less comfortable:
      a safety claim that nobody has tried to break is not evidence. It's a hope with
      better formatting.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Core Assertions</h2>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>1. A Safety Claim Must Be Falsifiable</h3>
      <p>
        "This system is safe" is not a claim; it's a slogan. A real claim specifies
        what would have to happen for it to be wrong, and someone goes and tries to
        make that happen. If a claim survives contact with an adversary who is
        actually trying to kill it, that's evidence. If no one tried, it's marketing.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>2. Alignment &ne; Safety</h3>
      <p>
        Alignment describes intent matching at time <em>t</em>.
        Safety is a property of behavior over <strong>time</strong>.
        A system that is aligned right now may be unsafe tomorrow if it cannot handle
        the accumulation of state, context drift, and recursive interaction.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>3. The Unit of Failure Is a Causal Chain, Not a Model Output</h3>
      <p>
        A bad completion is not the interesting failure. The interesting failure is
        what happens next: who or what acts on it, what state changes, what becomes
        irreversible three steps downstream. Grading a single output and stopping
        there measures the wrong object&mdash;it's the difference between citing a
        symptom and tracing a mechanism.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>4. Embodiment Turns Information Into State</h3>
      <p>
        In a chat window, a bad decision is a string you can delete. In a body, a bad
        decision is a position taken, a resource spent, a trust relationship damaged&mdash;
        a fact about the world that doesn't unhappen because someone closed the tab.
        Physical systems accumulate state; small misalignments compound into outcomes
        that can't be rolled back.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>5. Peers and Organisations Make Failure Compositional</h3>
      <p>
        Safety measured one model, one turn, at a time misses what happens when
        models talk to models, or when a coordinated set of agents and humans
        pursues an objective over time. Failure in a multi-agent system is not the
        sum of its single-agent failures&mdash;it's a different object, with its own
        cascade dynamics, that single-agent benchmarks cannot see at all.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>6. The World Talks Back</h3>
      <p>
        A simulated or embodied environment doesn't just receive a model's decision;
        it responds&mdash;with resistance, scarcity, other agents, physics that doesn't
        negotiate. An apparatus that can only produce the failure the designer already
        expected isn't testing anything. The world has to be able to say no.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>7. Simulation Is Where Failure Should Happen on Purpose</h3>
      <p>
        Plausible failure modes belong in a lab before they belong in a deployment
        incident report. Embodied evaluation and the Forward Threat exercises run
        inside a simulation boundary specifically so a mistake costs a recorded
        trace, not a person or a piece of infrastructure&mdash;the aggression
        belongs to the instrument, never to the boundary itself.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>8. Optimization Is a Hazard</h3>
      <p>
        Recursive optimization without bounds creates runaway behavior,
        even when the objective is benign. A system that optimizes past its
        safety constraints is more dangerous than one that was never aligned at all.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>9. Refusal Is Not Enough</h3>
      <p>
        A system that refuses once but leaks capability later is unsafe.
        Refusal quality matters: does the system hold its refusal across
        multi-turn interactions, temporal reframing, and authority escalation?
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>10. Graceful Failure Is the Baseline, Not the Summit</h3>
      <p>Safe systems, at minimum:</p>
      <ul class="principles">
        <li>Halt when conditions exceed their competence</li>
        <li>Degrade predictably under pressure</li>
        <li>Ask for help when uncertain</li>
        <li>Roll back when they detect their own errors</li>
        <li>Remain interpretable under stress</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        None of this is sufficient against an adversary patient enough to wait for
        the system to trust it again. Graceful failure is the floor every system
        needs before anything else is worth testing&mdash;it is not, by itself, a
        defence against a strategic opponent.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>11. We Attack Our Own Explanations</h3>
      <p>
        Every interesting result gets an opponent whose job is to kill it&mdash;not
        just the finding, the <em>explanation</em> for the finding. A weird result
        earns a place in the record because it survived a hostile framing, a
        scrambled label, a counterfactual replay, not because it was weird.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>12. Evidence Ends in Replayable Receipts</h3>
      <p>
        A finding that can't be replayed from a hash-addressed trace, a fixed seed,
        or an archived transcript is a claim, not evidence. Presentation can polish a
        finding; it cannot manufacture the property it's presenting.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="manifesto-highlight">
    <h2>Design Principle</h2>
    <blockquote>
      Build systems that fail <strong>early</strong>, <strong>clearly</strong>,
      <strong>reversibly</strong>, and <strong>with humans back in the loop</strong>&mdash;
      and design the evaluation to survive an adversary patient enough to make the
      system fail on its own schedule, not yours.
    </blockquote>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Corollary</h2>
    <p>
      The most dangerous AI systems are not misaligned ones.
      They are <strong>confidently aligned systems that cannot recognize their own failure</strong>&mdash;
      and the most dangerous version of that system is one a patient adversary has
      learned to steer without ever needing it to look compromised.
    </p>
    <p>
      A system that knows it is uncertain and asks for help is safer than a system
      that proceeds with false confidence. Failure awareness&mdash;the capacity to detect,
      acknowledge, and respond to one's own failure&mdash;is the foundational safety property.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Implications for Evaluation</h2>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Measure failure, not just success</h3>
      <p>
        Current benchmarks reward correct answers. Failure-first evaluation rewards
        systems that fail well: halting when appropriate, degrading predictably,
        maintaining interpretability, and supporting human re-entry.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Test over time, not at a point</h3>
      <p>
        Safety is a temporal property. Single-turn evaluations miss multi-turn erosion,
        context drift, and accumulated authority. Evaluation must span episodes, not snapshots.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Test across agents, not just across turns</h3>
      <p>
        A campaign is not a longer conversation. It is intent, hypothesis, action,
        observation, and doctrine update repeating across many agents and rounds.
        Evaluation that stops at one model, one session, cannot see what a
        persistent, coordinated adversary produces&mdash;this is the current frontier
        of our own evaluation practice, not a solved problem we're reporting on.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Include the human in the loop</h3>
      <p>
        Embodied systems operate alongside humans. Evaluation that excludes
        human interaction misses the most critical failure modes: deference failures,
        authority confusion, and escalation breakdowns.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <WarningBox title="Research Context">
    <p>
      This manifesto describes a research orientation, not a product specification.
      It is intended to guide AI safety research toward failure-first evaluation
      as a complement to existing alignment approaches. Some assertions above
      (Forward Threat, campaign-scale evaluation) describe the programme's current
      falsifiable bets, not results we have already banked&mdash;see the
      <a href="/research/forward-threat/">Forward Threat Lab</a> page for which is which.
    </p>
  </WarningBox>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  blockquote {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-primary);
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    color: var(--fg);
    background: rgba(0, 210, 255, 0.03);
    border-radius: 0 4px 4px 0;
  }
</style>
